{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Patient’s Guarantee of maximum NHS waits with choice of provider; match NHS spending while ending micromanagement; create Free Schools, expand successful schools, more per‑pupil funding direct to schools; more police and visible policing, tougher sentencing, clampdown on antisocial behaviour and drugs; £8bn tax cuts including 6p fuel duty cut, higher pensioner allowances, abolish IR35; asylum reception centres with dedicated removals and presumptive refusals from safe countries; transport investment, consider 80mph limits, revive rail, charge foreign lorries; keep the pound and reject the euro; Foot and Mouth recovery plan. TEXT_G: Independence referendum and more powers for Holyrood; abolish tuition fees and cut early class sizes; free personal care, hire 1,500 nurses, halve inpatient waits; 1,000 more police; cut fuel duty by 10p/gal funded by a new 5p income tax band over £100k; remove Trident and negotiate NATO withdrawal; target 25% electricity from renewables by 2010; reopen/electrify lines, national concessionary travel.",
  "reasoning": "In 2001, voters prioritized health service performance, education standards, crime, taxes/cost of living (including fuel), transport, Europe/the euro, and immigration/asylum—issues addressed directly and repeatedly in TEXT_F with concrete UK‑wide pledges. TEXT_G devotes substantial emphasis to Scottish constitutional change and defence posture, making it less aligned with the broad British electorate’s immediate service, tax, crime, transport, Europe/euro, and asylum concerns.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
